# Ignored paths that `git worktree-link` must COPY into a worktree rather than
# symlink, because the worktree both reads and writes them.
#
# Linking one of these is the same mistake as linking `state.sqlite` — two
# checkouts scribbling on one file — but denying it is also wrong, because the
# worktree genuinely needs the contents. Copying is the only answer that is
# right in both directions.
#
# Syntax matches .worktreeignore: one glob per line, `#` for comments, a pattern
# containing `/` matches the whole relative path and one without matches any
# basename. Precedence is deny > copy > link, so anything also named in
# .worktreeignore stays out entirely.
#
# A copy is made once and then left alone — a worktree is expected to dirty it.
# `git worktree-link --force` refreshes one, discarding whatever was written.

# The module's own data directory, and the reason this file exists.
#
# `crates/mbbs`'s ignored tests read fifteen Btrieve data files, three .MCV
# message files and the MDF out of here, and the module writes back into it:
# `_INIT__WCCMMUD` fopens `tmp/wccmmud.log` (call 122) and `tmp/WCCRECOV.FLG`,
# and `Host::btrieve_file` installs a .DAT from its virgin copy the first time a
# board has never been played on. Two checkouts running that suite against one
# linked directory would race on all of it -- and the meter is a number the
# tests assert, so the failure would look like a code change.
tmp
